Z||000004||Hank is Here||03-29-2001||06:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by KRMFiero:<BR><B><BR>2. Yesturday we were going down a path 6-12" snow and we were crossing over a small pond and the ice cracked and it fell in (only the front) we were able to get it back but just it couldnt get traction on anything (Only 1 front wheel spins and 1 back wheel)<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Lockers, definetly lockers!   ||||reg||1
Z||000005||fierojunky||03-30-2001||10:44 AM||||Make sure he does his homework on lockers before he buys one though a locker makes both wheels turn at the same speed - it "locks" the differential, so that it is not possible for all power to go to the spinning wheel.  a locker will result in a more difficult ride on good roads (especially on salt or newly tar and chiped roads). A limited slip differential feels that one wheel is losing traction, and brings a certain percentage (in most cases around 75 %) to the other wheel, that has still gripp. So it works a lot "softer" then a real locker.  ARB makes an air activated locker which you can put on and of probably the best solution.  just my opinion ||||reg||1
